    relatedSlugs: buyLeadsRel,
    content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A contractor running Google Ads for the first time usually loses money in the first month, not because the platform doesn't work for home services, but because the account is set up in ways that quietly waste budget before a single lead ever shows up. The mistakes are predictable and almost all preventable with a little planning before launch." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Starting With Broad Match and No Negatives" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Broad match keywords paired with an empty negative keyword list is the single most expensive combination in Google Ads, since it lets the platform show ads for searches only loosely related to the service, job seekers, DIY researchers, and homeowners in cities the business doesn't even serve." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Sending Clicks to the Homepage" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A general homepage forces the visitor to hunt for the specific service they searched for, and that extra friction costs conversions. A dedicated landing page matching the ad's exact service and offer converts noticeably better than a generic site link ever will." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Ignoring Geographic Targeting Precision" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Targeting an entire metro area when the business only wants jobs within a 20-mile service radius means paying for clicks from prospects who can never become customers. Radius targeting around the actual service area keeps spend concentrated where it can convert." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Skipping Call Tracking From Day One" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Without call tracking numbers tied to specific campaigns, there's no way to know which keywords or ad groups actually produce phone calls, and decisions about where to increase or cut spend end up based on guesswork instead of real conversion data." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Writing Ads That Sound Like Everyone Else's" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Ad copy built entirely around generic claims, licensed, insured, quality work, blends into every competitor's listing. Ads that mention specific response times, guarantees, or service specialties earn more clicks because they give the searcher an actual reason to choose that listing." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Setting a Budget Too Small to Learn Anything" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A daily budget that gets exhausted by 9am means the campaign is invisible for the rest of the day, including evening hours when many homeowners search after work. Underfunded campaigns also take longer to gather the data needed to optimize bidding effectively." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Never Checking the Search Terms Report" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The search terms report shows the actual phrases triggering ads, and reviewing it weekly during the first months surfaces irrelevant queries fast. Businesses that never open this report keep paying for the same wasteful clicks month after month without realizing it." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Neglecting Call Extensions and Click-to-Call" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Most searches for emergency home service work happen on a phone, and an ad without a click-to-call extension forces that searcher to copy a number or tap through to a website first, adding friction that a one-tap call button removes entirely. New advertisers who skip this simple setup step lose calls to competitors whose ads let a frustrated, time-pressed searcher reach a human in a single tap, which matters most exactly when the lead is the most urgent and most valuable." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Turning Campaigns Off Too Early" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Google Ads campaigns typically need several weeks of data before the algorithm and the advertiser both understand what's working, and pausing or drastically changing a campaign after only a few days of underwhelming results usually throws away the learning period right before it would have paid off." },
      { type: "heading", text: "A Quick Setup Checklist" },
      { type: "list", items: [
        "Build a negative keyword list before the campaign goes live, not after.",
        "Use phrase or exact match for core service terms rather than broad match.",
        "Send traffic to a service-specific landing page, not the homepage.",
        "Install call tracking before spending the first dollar.",
      ]},
      { type: "heading", text: "What a Realistic Starting Budget Looks Like" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "New advertisers often ask for a single dollar figure, but the honest answer depends on the trade and market: a daily budget needs to be large enough to generate a meaningful number of clicks before the algorithm has enough data to optimize, and cutting the budget too thin to feel safe usually just stretches out the learning period without actually reducing risk. A rough rule of thumb is budgeting for at least a few dozen clicks per week per active ad group before judging performance." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Vetting an Agency or Freelancer Before Handing Over the Account" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Contractors who don't want to manage bids themselves often hire an agency or freelancer, and the vetting process matters as much as the setup checklist itself. Ask for examples of actual contractor accounts they've managed, not just screenshots of dashboards, and confirm the business retains ownership and admin access to its own Google Ads account rather than operating inside an agency-controlled account that can't be transferred later." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Red Flags That Signal a Management Problem" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A management arrangement that refuses to share raw account access, reports only high-level summaries instead of search terms and conversion data, or can't explain why a specific keyword or bid strategy is being used should raise concern. Transparency about what's actually happening inside the account is one of the clearest signals of whether a management relationship is worth continuing." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Getting the fundamentals right from the start saves months of wasted spend, and for contractors who want booked jobs without managing bids and negative keywords, [exclusive leads](/buy-leads) offer a more predictable alternative." },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: "How much should a new contractor budget to start on Google Ads?", a: "Enough to generate several dozen clicks per week per active ad group is a reasonable floor. Below that, the learning period stretches out and it becomes hard to tell whether the campaign is actually underperforming or simply under-fed with data." },
      { q: "How long before a new Google Ads campaign shows real results?", a: "Most campaigns need three to six weeks of consistent spend before the account has enough conversion data to optimize bidding meaningfully. Judging results after only a few days almost always leads to premature, wrong conclusions." },
      { q: "Should a contractor manage Google Ads themselves or hire help?", a: "It depends on available time and willingness to learn the platform. A contractor with a few hours a week and patience for the search terms report can manage a simple account; a busier owner is usually better served by a specialist who reports transparently and keeps the business in control of its own account." },
      { q: "Is Local Services Ads a better starting point than Google Ads search campaigns?", a: "Local Services Ads has a lower setup barrier and charges per lead rather than per click, which makes it more forgiving for beginners. Many contractors eventually run both, since they capture different parts of the search results page." },
      { q: "What's a warning sign that a Google Ads account is being mismanaged?", a: "Vague reporting, no access to the actual account, and an inability to explain specific keyword or bid decisions are the clearest signs. A well-run account should be an open book to the business paying for it." },
    ],
